Since {A} multiplied by {B} makes {D}, therefore {B} measures {D} according to the units in {A}. [!just VII.Def.15]

But the unit {F} also measures the number {A} according to the units in it, therefore the unit {F} measures the number {A} the same number of times that {B} measures {D}. Therefore the unit {F} is to the number {A} as {B} is to {D}. [!just VII.Def.20]

For the same reason the unit {F} is to the number {A} as {C} is to {E}, therefore {B} is to {D} as {C} is to {E}. [!just VII.Def.20; V.11]

Therefore, alternately {B} is to {C} as {D} is to {E}. [!just VII.13]

Therefore, *if a number multiplied by two numbers makes certain numbers, then the numbers so produced have the same ratio as the numbers multiplied.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

Symbolically, *b* : *c* = *ab* : *ac.*

This proposition is used very frequently in Books VII through IX starting with the next proposition.
